Stored credential connectivity check: headers only, shared transport budget
=========================================================================

The generic stored-credential HTTP check only needs an HTTP status. Previously
it downloaded and decoded the entire response before inspecting that status.
Large or compressed responses consumed unnecessary resources, malformed gzip
could turn a received status into a transport failure, and a server sending
headers slowly could extend the request despite the per-read timeout.

The endpoint now uses HTTPX's streaming response context, captures the status,
and closes the response without iterating or decoding the body. An absolute
ten-second asyncio deadline covers asynchronous outgoing transport and cleanup
across HTTPS and any explicitly enabled plaintext fallback. A fallback never
receives a fresh ten-second budget. Deadline expiry stops further attempts and
records a failed check with a generic message. Caller cancellation propagates;
this change does not introduce a broad BaseException handler.

This is not a ten-second deadline for the whole API operation. Authorization,
target validation and DNS resolution, secret decryption and database commits
remain separate work. Synchronous certificate-context construction cannot be
interrupted by asyncio. HTTP parser/header limits and global concurrency limits
are not changed, and no process-memory benchmark or hard memory ceiling is
claimed. Network buffers may receive body bytes with headers; application code
does not consume or decompress the response body.

Existing safeguards remain: validate and pin a private/on-prem target, ignore
ambient HTTP proxy routing, preserve private CA-file/directory trust, verify
certificates by default, do not follow redirects, and require explicit opt-in
for plaintext fallback. Receiving any complete HTTPS status ends the check;
an HTTP error or redirect never triggers plaintext fallback. A 2xx result means
HTTP connectivity only, not successful credential or vendor authentication.

The new regression cases use real PostgreSQL and owned TCP HTTP/TLS listeners
with synthetic credentials. Statuses 200, 401 and 503 are combined with finite
large, chunked, gzip and malformed-gzip bodies. The listener observes whether
the client closes before the body is offered. Empty responses, authorization
and forbidden-target denials are controls. Slow headers and an HTTPS timeout
with plaintext fallback exercise elapsed time and the shared budget.

Twenty-nine cases reproduce fifteen old failures with fourteen controls. The
related backend suite includes the existing TLS, proxy and permission tests.
A separate before/after probe sends actual incoming TCP HTTP requests rather
than using the ASGI regression transport. Each request checks all 51 existing
credential rows. No HTTPX, DNS or security validator is replaced in these new
cases. Exact elapsed observations are retained; comparisons between separate
runs compare semantic outcomes and assert timing bounds independently.

Existing HTTP-outcome and TLS-default test doubles implement the streaming
context now; their outcome, certificate-policy and proxy-isolation assertions
are preserved. Frontend and OpenAPI contracts are unchanged. Local and shipping
qualification have separate source bindings, and local success does not imply
shipping completion. No customer hardware operations, real login validation,
concurrent authority-revocation guarantee or enterprise acceptance is claimed.
Hostname/SNI, IPv6 addressing and full vendor-specific credential verification
remain separate audit work.
